#!/usr/bin/env python3
# burrow-coverage: how much of Go's standard library burrow has, and a gate on
# the packages it says are finished.
#
#     tools/burrow-coverage                    one line per package, then a total
#     tools/burrow-coverage sync context       just those
#     tools/burrow-coverage -v unicode/utf8    every declaration, with its C name
#     tools/burrow-coverage --missing sync     only what is not there yet
#
# It reads every declaration in $GOROOT/api/go1*.txt, works out the C name that
# the rules in docs/design/08-naming-abi.md section 1 give it, and looks for that
# name in burrow's public headers. The declarations are counted the same way as
# tools/inventory.sh: a line that only exists on some platforms counts if it
# exists on one of the targets, and a line on all of them counts once.
#
# Each declaration comes out as one of four things. Present means the name is
# declared. Missing means it is not. Waived means tools/coverage-waivers.txt
# says why it never will be, in the form Go spells it. Renamed means the name
# rules say one thing and the headers use another on purpose, and the table
# RENAMED below says which.
#
# THE GATE
#
# tools/coverage-done.txt lists the packages that are finished. For those, a
# missing declaration that is not waived is an error and the exit status is 1.
# Every other package is reported and nothing more. A package goes in the file
# in the same pull request that finishes it, so from then on nothing in it can
# be removed or renamed by accident.
#
# WHAT IT CHECKS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
#
# A name is present if it is a token in the headers once comments and strings
# are gone. A struct field is present if the struct with the mapped name has a
# member with the mapped field name, and an interface method is present if the
# vtable has a member with that name or there is a function with the method's
# name.
#
# A function or method that is declared as a prototype also has its signature
# checked against the shape the rules give it, which is:
#
#   - the receiver first, as a value or a pointer the way Go has it
#   - optionally an Alloc * after it, for anything that allocates
#   - the parameters in Go's order, with a slice or a variadic as Slice
#   - the first result as the return value, and void or bool if there is none,
#     bool being for a call that can fail to allocate where Go's cannot
#   - every further result as a pointer on the end, in order, and an error
#     last of all as Error *; or, for three results besides the error, a struct
#     whose name ends in Ret
#
# That is the section "More than one result" in docs/guides/conventions.md,
# written as a program.
#
# A signature that is not that shape is reported as differs, and in a finished
# package that fails the gate like a missing name does. Where a header departs
# from the shape on purpose, tools/coverage-reshaped.txt says why, in the same
# form as the waivers. A name that is only a macro has no prototype to read and
# is not compared, and neither are generic functions, whose C side is a macro
# by rule R12.
#
# It needs Python 3 and a Go toolchain, or GOROOT pointing at a Go tree.
